    "lawCode": "Add §391-cc, Gen Bus L",
    "lawSection": "General Business Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7503B\n\nSPONSOR: CLEARE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general business law, in relation to requiring\nretailers of micromobility devices, bicycles with electric assist and\nlimited use motorcycles powered by lithium-ion batteries, and lithium-\nion batteries intended for use in such devices or bicycles to provide\ncustomers with an operating manual\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1. The general business law is amended by adding a new section\n391.\n\n§ 2. Effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTo assist in the prevention of overcharging and explosion of batteries\nfor lithium-ion-battery-powered e-bikes, e-scooters, other micromobility\ndevices, limited use motorcycles, or the lithium-ion batteries for such.\nThis legislation requires that retailers of these products supply the\ncustomer with an operating manual that includes instructions on storing\nand charging batteries and management when problems arise.\n\nHere are just a few of the fires in New York City caused by e-bike and\ne-scooter batteries last year, which reached a record of 104 fires,\nresulting in 79 injuries and four deaths. These numbers more than\ndoubled from the previous year, when 44 fires led to 23 injuries, up\nfrom 28 fires in 2019.\n\nAn e-bike's battery burst into flames while charging in a Bronx restau-\nrant, starting a four-alarm fire that drew more than 150 first respon-\nders and left one firefighter seriously injured.\n\nA man was killed, and multiple families displaced when the nine e-bike\nbatteries he was charging erupted inside his apartment. The force of the\nblast blew out the windows and collapsed an internal wall, according to\nthe FDNY.\n\nAn E-scooter battery fire tore through a house end killed a 9-year-old\nboy.\n\nFind information on E-bikes and E-scooters and charging at\nhttps://rechargedcommute.com/\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n180 Days.\n",
